import{_ as t,o as a,c as r,ag as o}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Review and pull requests","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"agentic/review.md","filePath":"agentic/review.md"}'),s={name:"agentic/review.md"};function i(n,e,h,d,l,c){return a(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="review-and-pull-requests" tabindex="-1">Review and pull requests <a class="header-anchor" href="#review-and-pull-requests" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Review and pull requests&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A change ends in a pull request, and a pull request ends in a merge. Between the two, the change gets a last check of its diff, a review by a context that did not write it, CI on its head, and your decision. This page describes the steps from a finished branch to a merged pull request, the agents of each step, and the rule of who merges.</p><h2 id="before-the-pull-request" tabindex="-1">Before the pull request <a class="header-anchor" href="#before-the-pull-request" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Before the pull request&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A builder that has a PASS does not open the pull request at once. It first runs <a href="./../components/agents#julia-branch-verifier"><code>julia-branch-verifier</code></a> on the branch. The verifier finds the diff against the merge base and runs a pass of numbered points on the diff only, for example correctness, type piracy and ambiguities with the <a href="./static-checks#what-a-change-adds-to-the-method-table">dispatch delta</a>, type instability, avoidable allocations, stale comments, and history outside the <code>CHANGELOG.md</code>. It fixes what it can, and returns three things:</p><ul><li>a verdict;</li><li>the exact paths to stage, so that the builder commits nothing that it did not change;</li><li>a block for the body of the pull request, with what the verifier checked.</li></ul><p>A finding counts against the branch only when its source is in the changed lines, or when the branch made it wrong. A finding that the branch does not cause is reported, not fixed, so the diff holds the change and nothing else. A finding that the branch causes and the verifier cannot fix stops the pull request until it is fixed and the verifier has run again.</p><p>The verifier does not run the suite: the loop ran it beside the critic. For a missing changelog entry it names <a href="./../components/agents#changelog-scribe"><code>changelog-scribe</code></a>, which writes the entry in the convention of the repository.</p><h2 id="the-text-of-a-pull-request" tabindex="-1">The text of a pull request <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-text-of-a-pull-request" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The text of a pull request&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A pull request is read on GitHub, by a reader who does not have your machine. So its text:</p><ul><li>holds no local path, such as a path in your home directory or a task file;</li><li>holds no closing keyword before an issue number, such as &quot;fixes #12&quot; or &quot;closes #12&quot;, unless the part is to close that issue: the keyword closes the issue on the merge, even inside a sentence that denies it;</li><li>holds the verifier&#39;s block, and the evidence of each clause.</li></ul><p><code>gh pr create</code> needs <code>-R &lt;owner&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;</code>: the <a href="./../safeguards/guard-hooks#the-gh-pr-create-check">guard</a> refuses one that names no repository, and asks for one outside your own organisations.</p><h2 id="the-review" tabindex="-1">The review <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-review" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The review&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A pull request that the loop opened has passed a blind critic already. A pull request from elsewhere, or one that you want reviewed again, goes to the review agents:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">agent or command</th><th style="text-align:left;">does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="./../components/agents#julia-pr-reviewer"><code>julia-pr-reviewer</code></a></td><td style="text-align:left;">reviews the pull request in a worktree at its head, measures each figure that it claims again, reads the CI jobs, and posts the review. It edits no code</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="./../components/agents#julia-pr-shepherd"><code>julia-pr-shepherd</code></a></td><td style="text-align:left;">has <code>julia-pr-reviewer</code> review, fixes the findings of severity <code>blocker</code>, <code>bug</code> and <code>correctness-risk</code>, pushes, and answers each finding in one comment. It makes one fix round and never merges</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><a href="./../components/commands#review-pr"><code>/review-pr</code></a></td><td style="text-align:left;">starts the shepherd, or with <code>nofix</code> the reviewer, and waits for the CI runs of the push</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The review is blind in the same sense as the critic: the reviewer treats the author&#39;s description of the change as a claim to check, not as a fact. The shepherd does not wait for CI. Its report names the runs of its push, and its caller waits for them, because a sub-agent that waits for minutes must write its whole context to the cache again.</p><h3 id="comments-of-a-review-bot" tabindex="-1">Comments of a review bot <a class="header-anchor" href="#comments-of-a-review-bot" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Comments of a review bot&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Where CodeRabbit reviews the pull request, <code>build-part</code> answers each of its comments before the merge. A comment that names a defect goes to a fix round, like a critic&#39;s blocking defect. A comment that is wrong, or a matter of taste, is rejected with the reason. An inline comment gets its answer in its thread, <code>fixed in &lt;sha&gt;</code> or <code>rejected: &lt;reason&gt;</code>; the comments of a review body get one comment on the pull request. A defect that CodeRabbit finds in its review of the fix goes to you, not to another fix round.</p><h2 id="who-merges" tabindex="-1">Who merges <a class="header-anchor" href="#who-merges" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Who merges&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>No agent merges a pull request that its own session opened or pushed to.</strong> A session that writes a change, opens its pull request and merges it is a direct push to <code>main</code> with extra steps: no second context has said yes. The line is authorship, not time: a sub-agent that the authoring session spawned is on the author&#39;s side.</p><p>The harness holds the line in three places:</p><ul><li><strong>The permission settings</strong> put <code>gh pr merge</code> on the <code>ask</code> list, so every merge by an agent shows you a prompt (<a href="./../safeguards/permissions">Permissions</a>).</li><li><strong>The agents</strong> of the loop never merge: the builders, the critics and the verifier. The shepherd never merges either. The reviewer may merge a pull request that it reviewed, once the required checks are green, only on your explicit go-ahead, and never one that the session that started it opened.</li><li><strong><a href="./../components/commands#merge-pr"><code>/merge-pr</code></a></strong> is your explicit go-ahead to merge, and it still refuses a pull request that its session opened or pushed to. Otherwise it waits for the required checks, merges with <code>gh pr merge --merge --delete-branch</code>, removes the worktree and the local branch, and records the merge commit in the task file.</li></ul><p>A session that only reviewed a pull request may merge it, once its required checks are green and you have said so in that session. In most cases you merge yourself, on GitHub.</p><h2 id="after-the-merge" tabindex="-1">After the merge <a class="header-anchor" href="#after-the-merge" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;After the merge&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The worktree of the part goes, with <code>git worktree remove</code> from the main checkout, and the local branch with <code>git branch -d</code>. The task file records the merge, and the part&#39;s state becomes <code>merged</code>. A part that waits on this one starts only now, in a worktree from the new <code>main</code>. Parts that only add lines to a shared <code>CHANGELOG.md</code> rebase (<a href="./loop#several-parts-at-once">The builder-critic loop</a>).</p>',24)])])}const g=t(s,[["render",i]]);export{p as __pageData,g as default};
